  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contracting in Wyoming through FY2026 to date shows concentrated, construction-led spending. The state received $537M in contract obligations across 2,242 awards. The average award size was $240K, and obligations were up 8.1% year over year. The Department of the Interior accounted for the largest share of buying agency obligations at $314M, followed by the Department of Defense with $84.4M and the Department of Veterans Affairs with $42M. Construction-related industries dominated the award mix: Water and Sewer Line and Related Structures Construction totaled $246M, Highway, Street, and Bridge Construction totaled $62.9M, and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction totaled $58M. RECORD STEEL AND CONSTRUCTION, INC. and G.S.E. CONSTRUCTION COMPANY, INC. were the largest vendors with $129M and $109M in obligations respectively. Fiscal-year trends show a rise from $390M in FY21 to $559M in FY22, then fluctuation to $470M in FY23, $532M in FY24, $497M in FY25, and $537M in FY26. This profile highlights heavy federal investment in infrastructure and a small set of vendors capturing large shares of obligations.",
    "sections": [
